# Dependensi scratch untuk script diagnostik (scripts/db-baseline.mjs).
# Bukan dependensi runtime aplikasi — jangan ikut ter-commit.
.workbuddy-ai/

# Local environment variables (secrets)
.env.local
superbase.txt

# Scratch credential/handoff notes. These exist so secrets can be moved between
# a developer machine and a password manager WITHOUT passing through a commit.
# Nothing in here is ever source, and a token that reaches git history is
# permanent.
asj-astro.txt
*-credentials.txt
*-token*.txt

# The `netlify` CLI writes its token as a literal assignment string when told to
# (e.g. `netlify env:set NETLIFY_AUTH_TOKEN=...` accidentally redirected to a
# file), producing names like `netlify_auth_token=nfp_....txt`. These are NOT
# source. Covers the exact shape seen on 2026-09-11 as well as any regression to
# the same pattern.
netlify_auth_token*
*_auth_token*
*nfp_*
# GitHub PATs pasted into scratch files (2026-09-13: a full 40-char `ghp_…` token
# was found unlabelled on line 3 of netlify_auth_token=nfp_….txt — gitignored, so
# it never reached the remote, but the pattern deserves its own guard). Covers
# `ghp_`, `gho_`, `ghu_`, `ghs_`, `ghr_` and fine-grained `github_pat_`.
*ghp_*
*gho_*
*ghu_*
*ghs_*
*ghr_*
*github_pat_*

# ── Bundled credentials ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Firebase service account is shipped as a FILE bundled with the functions
# (netlify.toml `included_files`) instead of an env var, because AWS Lambda
# compatibility mode caps the total per-function environment at 4 KB and this
# single value was ~2.4 KB — 59 % of the budget, which failed the deploy at
# function creation. See docs/HANDOFF_4KB_ENV_LIMIT.md.
#
# The real file must NEVER be committed: it is a live private key, and git
# history is permanent. `.example` is committed by negation so the required
# shape is documented and a missing file is obvious rather than mysterious.
netlify/functions/secrets/*
!netlify/functions/secrets/*.example.json
!netlify/functions/secrets/README.md

# graft's local graph cache — regenerable, not committed (run `graft build`).
/graft/

# Output of scripts/ui-audit.mjs (screenshots + measurement report).
# Evidence, not source — regenerate with `node scripts/ui-audit.mjs`.
.tmp-audit/

# Ad-hoc measurement scripts written during a review session. These MUST be
# ignored, and not merely for tidiness: `indexer/src/discover.ts` does NOT skip
# dotfiles, so a `.tmp-*.mjs` in the repo root is counted as project code and
# silently shifts the file/mjs ratchets in discover.test.ts and build.test.ts.
# That is exactly how `mjs` once got frozen at 43 instead of 30 and the total at
# 404 instead of 391 — twice, despite a comment warning about it. Ignoring the
# pattern makes the matcher skip them, so the class of error cannot recur.
# See docs/UI_DESIGN_REVIEW.md §12.7.
.tmp-*

# Do NOT add a `!.tmp-audit/` negation here. In gitignore the LAST matching
# pattern wins, so a negation does not "keep the earlier rule" — it undoes it.
# While that line was present, `.tmp-audit/` (line 71) was cancelled and 13 MB of
# audit screenshots + admin-audit.json came back as untracked files that
# `git add -A` would have committed. Measured at the time:
#   git check-ignore -v .tmp-audit/admin-audit.json  ->  exit 1 (NOT ignored)
# and after removing it:
#   git check-ignore -v .tmp-audit/admin-audit.json  ->  .gitignore:81:.tmp-*
# That line number is the point worth remembering: check-ignore reports only the
# LAST matching rule, so `.tmp-audit/` on line 71 still matches but never appears
# in the output. A negation therefore looks like a harmless restatement of the
# rule above while actually being the only rule that decides.
# The two rules are not redundant, they are sequential: `.tmp-audit/` names the
# one directory that is evidence, `.tmp-*` is the catch-all that keeps scratch
# probes out of the indexer ratchets.
